A||||9||86SE||Fricken code 32!  It came BACK!  Gokart can you help?||||8||
Z||000000||86SE||07-06-2000||11:20 PM||||I just replaced my EGR calve because the old one broke (no codes with the old one broke, it was hanging by the thin metal rod in the middle of it).<P>A few months ago I bought a new EGR solenoid, and that cured the FIRST code 32 problem.<P>So last weekend I was coming home, testing out my new EGR valve.  It was working fine, idled perfectly at about 1000 RPM.  Then I launched it hard and took it to 5K.  Then about 2 min later, the Service Engine light poped on.  Checked it when I got home and it was the code 32!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>Next morning I riped all the tape off the spots I broke the hose previously (putting on the EGR solenoid).  Put super glue on all the broken spots, then looked over at the egr valve and the little rubber boot that the vaccume line comes in was split.<P>I glued it back together and then put nylon ties on it.  Drove it, it heated up, the light came on again!  Went over all the crackes with 5 min appoxy.  I dont think it can leak, but the code still comes up.<P>And I did clear the ECM a few times too.<P>Any ideas??<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Fiero||||reg||8
Z||000001||ROB MAC||07-06-2000||11:26 PM||||Stop using glue and replace the split, cracked, broken stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000002||86SE||07-06-2000||11:39 PM||||OH come on, im CHEAP!  What can I tell ya?  hehehe<P>Heck, the tape hasent failed me yet, it won't stop now.  I'll use tape.||||reg||11
Z||000003||wkayl||07-07-2000||12:01 AM||||Here is how I fixed my code 32....<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/code_1.jpg">  <P>The 1st week in June I made a 1500 mile trip.  About 300 miles from home the check enging light came on.  I checked codes and it was 32.  I replaced the EGR valve but it didn't help.  I took the EGR solenoid off and knocked it around a bit.  I thought I had it for the code didn't return.  Last weekend I took that same 1500 mile trip and 25 miles into it the light came on again. Being I was driving at night I came up with the fix above.  I picked up some WD-40 and soaked it inside and out.  No luck that evening.  The next day and the next 500 miles it stayed off.  Then.....blink, on it came.  Yesterday I took the solenoid apart completly and cleaned it.  I havn't had a chance to try it out at highway speeds yet but I'm going to Denver on Sunday and will find out then.<p>[This message has been edited by wkayl (edited 07-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gokart||07-07-2000||06:10 AM||||I believe it was wxxo that solved my EGR woes for me. His instructions went like this 1) buy some electrical tape 2) cut a small square about the same size as the opening of the "check engine light" window 3) Oh....you know the rest! I changed the vavle, solenoid, checked for vaccumm leaks, checked all vaccumm lines etc etc etc but I still get it when I hit 85mph. I can't tell the difference though cause shes running sweet...so F*** IT! The biggest mystery for me is for the first 6 months I had my GT the plastic tube going from the solenoid to the firewall metal tube wasn't connected and I never got code 32  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> It wasn't till I had Cobra over and he discovered it and then connected them! If it's not affecting your performance like in my case I wouldn't worry about it. Good luck!<P>------------------<BR> Marc                     87GT<p>[This message has been edited by Gokart (edited 07-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||wxxo||07-07-2000||11:30 AM||||did someone say my name haha. yup thats the best way to fix that damn egr. i get a little emotional talking about it you know. that black tape works really well. however i have found a new use for it. it works just as well for all of us with manual trannys. that damn shift light is quite annoying too! little bit of tape...problem solved! now i can redline it in every gear. thats right, every gear. i wonder if that black tape will fix a cracked block too. well we'll all have to tune in later to see how wxxo has jury rigged his fiero this time using standard electrical tape. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||tgowens||07-07-2000||11:54 AM||||wkayl, Gokart and wxxo, I have used the "tape fix" many times too, especially on long trips (driven from KY to CA and back twice) but I have found that removing the tape at night is the answer to a long trip -- it helps when you have your "night light" on to keep you from falling asleep at the wheel. So, 86SE, if you make a "fix" for it, make sure that it isn't a permenant "super-glue" type but rather a removable type.......<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/my_fiero_sig.gif">   <BR>'87 Fiero GT -  <A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero/" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A>||||reg||1
Z||000007||86SE||07-07-2000||01:38 PM||||Not a bad idea!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I'm going to still work on it, I know I can get it to go away.  I did it once, I can do it again.  If I can't i'll use the black tape trick, if I can, I'll post what I did to fix it.<P>PS - You have to use RED tape for a cracked block.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Fiero||||reg||3
Z||000008||triker||07-07-2000||02:15 PM||||The line going to the steel tube against the firewall is just a filtered vent line, that's why it doesn't cause a code 32.  Most GM cars leave it open to the engine comp.  A temp fix to that pesky light, that doesn't involve black tape, is to hook the ported vacuum line (the bottom of the 2 that plug into the solenoid) to the vacuum port on the little box attached to the EGR solenoid with a piece of vacuum line.  It's a simple vacuum operated switch that will fool the ECM into thinking everything is fine.||||reg||12
Z||000009||Gokart||07-08-2000||05:21 AM||||I'll try this! The only thing that was concerning me when that bloody light came on was "what if it's not the egr this time" <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> This will solve that <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> It won't do any harm to the solenoid or valve...right?<P>------------------<BR> Marc                     87GT||||reg||2
